---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { data, gates, format } from "../lib/data.js";
---

<Base title="How the benchmark works">
  <div class="wrap page-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">The method, explained</p>
    <h1>What are we measuring?</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      The time between submitting a document change and observing the requested
      state through the database’s public APIs.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="wrap article-layout">
    <aside class="contents" aria-label="On this page">
      <p>On this page</p>
      <a href="#changes">The three changes</a>
      <a href="#clock">How the clock works</a>
      <a href="#reading-results">Reading the results</a>
      <a href="#checks">Quality and validity</a>
      <a href="#limits">Scope and limitations</a>
    </aside>
    <article class="prose">
      <section id="changes">
        <h2>Three everyday changes</h2>
        <dl class="definition-list">
          <dt>Insert</dt>
          <dd>
            Add a new document. It must be present in a direct read and appear
            with its new version in vector search.
          </dd>
          <dt>Replace</dt>
          <dd>
            Keep the document ID, but replace its content and vector. Finding
            the old version does not count. Before the write, the target must be
            absent from search with its new vector.
          </dd>
          <dt>Delete</dt>
          <dd>
            Remove a previously retrievable document. It must become absent in
            both direct reads and repeated searches with its old vector. A
            missing search hit alone is insufficient.
          </dd>
        </dl>
      </section>
      <section id="clock">
        <h2>A clock that starts with the application</h2>
        <p>
          We start the clock when the client submits a change. Separate readers
          begin before the write acknowledgement and check the direct-ID and
          vector-search APIs independently.
        </p>
        <div class="reading-callout compact">
          <h3>Two useful milestones</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>First visible:</strong> the later of the first correct
            response on each read channel. Both channels have shown the target
            state by that time, although they were not sampled at the same
            instant.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Repeated confirmation:</strong> each channel has returned at
            least three correct responses spanning 50 ms. Incorrect responses
            and read errors reset that channel’s confirmation sequence.
          </p>
        </div>
        <p>
          Confirmation includes its observation window. The result also includes
          client scheduling, serialization, transport, and query execution. It
          does not isolate time inside the index.
        </p>
        <p>
          A change has five seconds to succeed. Late responses stay in the
          evidence but cannot establish success. Each read channel stops after
          confirmation, so this finite window cannot prove permanent visibility.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section id="reading-results">
        <h2>How to read a result</h2>
        <h3>What does p95 mean?</h3>
        <p>
          Within a trial, the 95th percentile (p95) is a time at or below which
          95% of successful measurements fall. It helps describe the slower end
          of the measurements without relying on a single maximum.
        </p>
        <p>
          Each engine and workload has five trials. The comparison shows the{" "}
          <strong>median of those five trial p95s</strong>. It is not the p95 of
          every event pooled together.
        </p>
        <h3>What is the 95% interval?</h3>
        <p>
          We resample whole trials 5,000 times to form a bootstrap interval
          around the estimate. Five trials give coarse uncertainty. The interval
          does not establish statistical significance or a universal winner.
        </p>
        <h3>Why show confirmation rates?</h3>
        <p>
          Latency numbers describe successful changes. The confirmation rate
          uses all offered changes, including errors, timeouts, and client
          drops. A short time is not useful on its own if many changes fail.
        </p>
        <h3>How is search quality checked?</h3>
        <p>
          One hundred real questions query an unchanged part of the same index
          while mutations target separate document IDs. Approximate search is
          compared with exhaustive cosine search. At least 95% recall is
          required before and during mutations. Equal-score neighbors at the
          tenth boundary receive credit within a 10⁻⁶ tolerance.
        </p>
        <p>
          The separate nDCG@10 score uses relevance judgments from the dataset.
          These are scores for declared subsets, not full BEIR leaderboard
          scores. Quality queries are filtered to the fixed cohort; mutation
          probes search the whole collection.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section id="checks">
        <h2>What makes a comparison valid?</h2>
        <p>
          Every planned trial must finish and pass all eight checks. Failed
          trials and failed checks stay visible.
        </p>
        <ul>
          {Object.values(gates).map((label) => (
            <li>{label}.</li>
          ))}
        </ul>
        <details>
          <summary>Exact timing and index rules</summary>
          <p>
            Arrival and background-query dispatch p95 must not exceed their
            requested interarrival interval. Probe dispatch p95 must be at most
            10 ms. Positive probes are spaced at least 25 ms apart; negative
            probes at least 10 ms apart.
          </p>
          <p>
            All engines use cosine similarity and HNSW parameters m=16,
            construction effort 128, and search effort 128. PostgreSQL records
            an HNSW query plan with sequential scans disabled. Weaviate uses a
            zero flat-search cutoff. Qdrant requires at least 95% indexed base
            vectors; remaining small segments can still use scans.
          </p>
        </details>
        <h3>We also test the measurement harness</h3>
        <p>
          Known-delay controls inject visibility delays of 0, 25, and 100 ms. A
          separate 100 ms acknowledgement delay checks that readers are
          independent of write acknowledgement.
        </p>
        <div class="table-scroll">
          <table>
            <caption>
              Known-delay controls from the reference experiment
            </caption>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Visibility delay</th>
                <th>Acknowledgement delay</th>
                <th>Confirmed changes</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {data.calibration.map((c) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>{c.injected_visibility_ms} ms</td>
                  <td>{c.injected_ack_ms} ms</td>
                  <td>
                    {c.statuses.success}/{c.total_operations}
                  </td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section id="limits">
        <h2>Where these results apply</h2>
        <p>
          This reference uses one Apple M4 host, Docker Desktop, and pinned
          images. Each database gets two virtual CPUs and 3 GiB of memory.
          Trials use fresh collections, five repetitions, 30-second measured
          phases, ten changes/s, and ten background queries/s. Bursts offer 40
          changes/s for one second every ten seconds. Observation reads add
          traffic.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            The datasets supply real text and questions, but document changes
            are simulated on disjoint IDs.
          </li>
          <li>
            Static relevant-first subsets, one embedding model, and a filtered
            quality workload bound the conclusions.
          </li>
          <li>
            The client and containers share physical hardware. Other hardware
            and configurations define different comparison tracks.
          </li>
          <li>
            Qdrant uses wait=true, Weaviate uses synchronous indexing with REST
            writes and GraphQL searches, and PostgreSQL uses autocommit with
            independent connections. These are different API and acknowledgement
            semantics.
          </li>
          <li>
            The experiment does not establish maximum throughput, durability,
            distributed consistency, or performance during long-running churn.
          </li>
          <li>
            An earlier host interruption retained six complete trials and
            restarted a preserved partial seventh attempt. A later user pause
            followed trial 44 at a clean boundary. Calibration and environment
            checks were repeated; container restarts may affect cache state.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Independent performance replication on another host remains future
          work. All raw observations and continuation records are available to
          inspect.
        </p>
        <h3>Related work</h3>
        <p>
          <a href="https://github.com/erikbern/ann-benchmarks">
            ANN-Benchmarks
          </a>
          ,{" "}
          <a href="https://github.com/zilliztech/VectorDBBench">
            VectorDBBench
          </a>
          , <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.09613">FreshDiskANN</a>, and{" "}
          <a href="https://github.com/beir-cellar/beir">BEIR</a> provide
          important context. This project makes no claim to be the first
          freshness benchmark.
        </p>
        <a
          class="text-link"
          href="https://github.com/aadithya2112/freshness-benchmark/blob/v0.5.0/methodology.md"
        >
          Read the full versioned protocol →
        </a>
      </section>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>
